WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.176
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.476 --> 00:00:08.944
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:14.775
Unit thirty: Modals of Deduction (must / can't). Today you will make logical guesses from evidence.

4
00:00:15.075 --> 00:00:17.553
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:21.501
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:23.374
Must be.

7
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:26.824
Must be means I am almost sure it is true.

8
00:00:27.417 --> 00:00:28.743
He must be tired.

9
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:32.107
Your turn. Say: Must be.

10
00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:36.119
Must be.

11
00:00:36.883 --> 00:00:37.873
Can't be.

12
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:41.521
Can't be means I am almost sure it is not true.

13
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:43.443
That can't be right.

14
00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:46.865
Your turn. Say: Can't be.

15
00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:50.620
Can't be.

16
00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:52.257
Might be.

17
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.052
Might be means it is possible.

18
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:56.837
She might be at home.

19
00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:59.777
Your turn. Say: Might be.

20
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:03.553
Might be.

21
00:01:04.317 --> 00:01:05.666
Must have been.

22
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.300
Must have been means I am sure about the past.

23
00:01:09.883 --> 00:01:11.511
It must have been difficult.

24
00:01:12.117 --> 00:01:14.618
Your turn. Say: Must have been.

25
00:01:17.467 --> 00:01:18.654
Must have been.

26
00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:20.580
Can't have been.

27
00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:24.082
Can't have been means I am sure it did not happen.

28
00:01:24.683 --> 00:01:25.963
He can't have been there.

29
00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.005
Your turn. Say: Can't have been.

30
00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:33.007
Can't have been.

31
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:34.658
Certain.

32
00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:37.324
Certain means completely sure.

33
00:01:37.917 --> 00:01:38.999
I am certain.

34
00:01:39.583 --> 00:01:42.270
Your turn. Say: Certain.

35
00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:46.030
Certain.

36
00:01:46.783 --> 00:01:47.726
Evidence.

37
00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:51.401
Evidence means facts that show something is true.

38
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:53.575
The evidence is clear.

39
00:01:54.183 --> 00:01:56.476
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

40
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:00.392
Evidence.

41
00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:02.105
Deduction.

42
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:05.673
Deduction means a conclusion from evidence.

43
00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:07.679
This is my deduction.

44
00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:10.936
Your turn. Say: Deduction.

45
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.731
Deduction.

46
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:18.952
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:19.817 --> 00:02:23.061
Visal, look at someone near you. Make a deduction.

48
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:32.480
She must be a student, because she has a school bag. I use must when the evidence is strong, so I am almost certain.

49
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:35.258
And when the evidence says no?

50
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:45.077
He can't be a teacher, because he looks very young. Can't means I am almost sure it is not true. Do not use mustn't for a deduction.

51
00:02:46.183 --> 00:02:47.556
What about the past?

52
00:02:48.650 --> 00:03:01.025
Modal + have + past participle: It must have been a long journey. She can't have seen us. Might have is weaker: He might have missed the bus. The more evidence, the stronger the modal.

53
00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.004
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:04.850 --> 00:03:09.369
Grammar focus: Deduction: must be and can't be and might be.

55
00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:10.821
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:14.712
She must be a student; she has a school bag.

57
00:03:15.617 --> 00:03:18.266
He can't be a teacher; he looks too young.

58
00:03:19.150 --> 00:03:20.859
It must have been a long journey.

59
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:31.505
Must = almost sure it's true. Can't = almost sure it's not true. Might = possible. For the past use modal + have + past participle.

60
00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.344
Be careful! Don't say: He mustn't be at home.

61
00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.265
Say: He can't be at home.

62
00:03:39.565 --> 00:03:41.262
Use can't for deduction.

63
00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:44.870
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:45.667 --> 00:03:47.144
Reading the Evidence.

65
00:03:47.444 --> 00:03:50.314
She must be a student because she has a school bag.

66
00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.124
He can't be a teacher because he looks so young.

67
00:03:55.133 --> 00:03:57.272
The streets are wet, so it must have rained.

68
00:03:58.267 --> 00:04:00.545
He might be at home, but I'm not certain.

69
00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:03.753
A good deduction needs evidence.

70
00:04:04.867 --> 00:04:09.124
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.914
He has a uniform. He blank be a soldier.

72
00:04:13.214 --> 00:04:16.299
A, must. B, can't. C, mustn't.

73
00:04:19.833 --> 00:04:21.392
The answer is A: must.

74
00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:25.922
She said she was in Phnom Penh. She blank be in Siem Reap.

75
00:04:26.222 --> 00:04:29.353
A, must. B, can't. C, has to.

76
00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.644
The answer is B: can't.

77
00:04:35.783 --> 00:04:38.157
The ground is wet. It blank rained.

78
00:04:38.457 --> 00:04:42.052
A, must have. B, must. C, can't.

79
00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:47.623
The answer is A: must have.

80
00:04:48.783 --> 00:04:50.609
Which means 'I am only guessing'?

81
00:04:50.909 --> 00:04:55.363
A, He must be home.. B, He might be home.. C, He can't be home..

82
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:00.969
The answer is B: He might be home..

83
00:05:02.117 --> 00:05:04.836
According to the reading, why is the street wet?

84
00:05:05.136 --> 00:05:08.964
A, it rained. B, a hose. C, a flood.

85
00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:14.360
The answer is A: it rained.

86
00:05:15.567 --> 00:05:18.765
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:19.617 --> 00:05:23.218
Look at a person near you and make a deduction using 'must' or 'can't'.

88
00:05:32.017 --> 00:05:33.587
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:33.887 --> 00:05:45.655
The man next to me must be a doctor because he is wearing a white coat. He can't be a student, because he looks about forty. I use must and can't because I have evidence.

90
00:05:45.955 --> 00:05:47.618
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:48.517 --> 00:05:54.675
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and deduction: must be and can't be and might be.

92
00:05:54.975 --> 00:05:59.566
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

93
00:05:59.866 --> 00:06:02.681
See you in Unit thirty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:06:02.981 --> 00:06:05.727
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

